I'm exploring use cases by personal agents (Grok Box, Muse, Hark, etc.). Today we have 4 examples in the:

Inbox & Daily Brief

category. Check out Roger in my bio for a 3K+ use case library and some templates of agent teams.

@cheneypiano typed one sentence into his AI agent: "Go through every open conversation across my inboxes and tell me who I've left hanging." It found 14 people waiting on him, and three were time-sensitive. If you run a shop, those are customers and suppliers waiting on an answer. Try that same sentence on your own inbox this week.
https://t.co/Z0dMsHXe4y

@keeganrj gets his whole day handed to him at 7:43 am every morning. Grok Bot reads his inbox, task list and calendar and sorts it into money waiting on him, questions only he can answer, things to review and bills coming due, plus a prep sheet for every meeting. His prompt tells it to draft replies but never send anything without his approval. He shared the full prompt in his post, so you can copy it.
https://t.co/DEqjJtEolx

@louise19plowman noticed a dentist confirmation, a school play and a delivery window were all in her inbox and none were on her calendar. So she built a free Grok Bot template that finds plans in your email and drafts the events; reply "add all" and they're on your calendar. If your family's plans live in email, this is an easy first job to hand off.
https://t.co/rBH6fWMhUs

One post with a tale of caution:

@david_logger let his agent triage his inbox on a Monday morning. It archived three emails from actual humans and flagged a newsletter as "high priority, requires immediate response." The newsletter was about sourdough. For the first few weeks, let your agent sort and suggest, and check what it archived before you trust it.
https://t.co/D9BMFsYPJ5

I'm setting up a workshop for 400+ real estate agents this morning. Let's make the world more productive.
